Exercise-Associated Muscle Cramp
Common Infections and Afflictions
Sudden painful muscle contraction, Visible and palpable muscle knot, Exertional trigger

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-012
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, severe pain and spasm in the right calf muscle.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing acute, agonizing pain and a hardened, immobile right calf following strenuous physical activity. The pain has persisted for 90 minutes and prevents any weight-bearing. Given the severity of the unilateral limb pain and physical induration, an urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to decisively rule out acute deep vein thrombosis (DVT), acute compartment syndrome, or acute arterial ischemia. Delaying this structured physical evaluation and differential diagnosis until standard consulting hours presents an unacceptable risk of limb or life-threatening progression, rigorously fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My calf muscle suddenly locked up into a rock-hard knot, and the pain is so intense I thought the muscle tore in half.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 90 minutes prior, immediately following a prolonged, intense 15-kilometer run in warm weather with profuse sweating. The patient experienced a sudden, intense, and exquisitely painful involuntary muscle contraction in the right gastrocnemius. The patient reports a visible, palpable hard knot in the affected muscle belly that refuses to release entirely. Transient, minor relief was temporarily achieved with self-applied passive stretching, but the severe spasm rapidly recurred upon resting. Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, previous history of thromboembolism, or recent long-haul travel.
Functional Status: Currently non-ambulatory due to severe pain and mechanical inability to flex the ankle normally.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress secondary to extreme pain.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 142/88 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoresis noted. Acutely uncomfortable, massaging the right calf and refusing to bear weight on the right leg.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal serum electrolytes and normal creatine kinase post-recovery would assist in confirming the diagnosis in recurrent, severe cases, but are not acutely indicated.
Physical Exam: Right lower extremity reveals a visibly distorted gastrocnemius muscle belly. Palpation identifies a discrete, exquisitely tender, rock-hard spasm in the medial head of the gastrocnemius. Surrounding tissue is not uniformly edematous. Gentle, passive dorsiflexion of the right ankle elicits extreme pain but slowly elongates the contracted fibers. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are bounding and equal bilaterally. Capillary refill is <2 seconds. No resting fasciculations noted. Neurological examination of the lower limb is entirely normal with intact sensation and reflexes. Homan's sign is unreliable here but localized pain is distinct from deep venous cord tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Empty water bottles visible; evidence of recent heavy exertion.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient took 400mg Ibuprofen 30 minutes ago with zero effect on the spasm.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Exercise-Associated Muscle Cramp - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out clinically by the sudden, instantaneous onset directly linked to peak exertion, the presence of a palpable focal muscle knot rather than diffuse calf swelling/erythema, and a low Wells score.
Acute Compartment Syndrome - Unlikely due to the lack of 'pain out of proportion' to passive stretch of uncontracted muscles, intact distal pulses and sensation, and the localized nature of the knot rather than tense, woody induration of the entire fascial compartment.
Gastrocnemius Tear (Tennis Leg) - Less likely as the pain fluctuates with the physical spasm and there is no palpable defect/gap in the muscle belly, though micro-tearing may be a concurrent sequela of the severe contraction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient presented with unrelenting, severe unilateral calf pain causing complete immobility, raising immediate clinical suspicion for an acute vascular event such as a DVT or evolving compartment syndrome. An urgent on-site clinical assessment was absolutely necessary to perform a targeted vascular and musculoskeletal evaluation. Direct physical examination confirmed intact perfusion and localized the pathology to an extreme, prolonged mechanical muscle spasm triggered by fatigue and potential electrolyte depletion. Immediate intervention with clinician-assisted prolonged stretching and oral rehydration provided acute relief. This essential rule-out of limb-threatening pathology and definitive physical treatment could not have safely been deferred until the next day, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed sustained, passive dorsiflexion of the ankle for 3 minutes until the spasm physically yielded. Instructed immediate oral rehydration utilizing an electrolyte-rich solution (e.g., oral rehydration salts or sports drink). Advised Paracetamol 1000mg strictly every 6 hours to manage the resultant delayed-onset muscle soreness.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent recurrence overnight, ensure you consume at least 1 liter of an electrolyte replacement fluid before bed. Plain water dilutes your remaining blood sodium further, which will trigger the nerve endings to fire and cause the muscle to violently cramp again while you sleep.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner or Physiotherapist in 3-5 days if significant pain persists, indicating a possible secondary muscle strain.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the entire leg becomes uniformly swollen, red, hot to the touch, or if you lose feeling or the ability to move your toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: This is a clinical diagnosis; if the pain transitions from a cramp to a persistent, constant ache with diffuse swelling, reassessment for DVT is mandatory.
Clinical Course: The acute cramp has resolved, but the muscle has sustained microscopic trauma from the extreme contraction. You will experience significant soreness and a 'bruised' feeling in the calf for 48-72 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you notice your urine turning the color of dark tea or cola, as this can indicate muscle breakdown products damaging the kidneys.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The muscle essentially ran out of fuel and hydration, causing the electrical signals to short-circuit and lock the muscle in an 'on' position. Pulling the muscle into a stretch physically breaks the electrical feedback loop, forcing the muscle fibers to disengage.
Actionable Steps: Apply a warm compress or heating pad to the calf for 15 minutes to increase blood flow and flush out lactic acid. Avoid vigorous exercise for 48 hours to allow the muscle fibers to heal.

SMS INFORMATION
Severe Muscle Cramp. Spasm released via stretching. Rest leg. Drink electrolyte fluids immediately. Expect muscle soreness for 2-3 days.
SMS EDUCATION
Use a heat pack to soothe the muscle. Avoid plain water; drink electrolytes. Go to ED immediately if the whole leg swells, turns red, or if urine becomes dark brown.

